sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch11-14> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Throughout the world, plants growing on high mountains (including tropical ones) and on the temperate lowlands of the northern and southern hemispheres are sometimes identical species but much more often distinct yet closely related species.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Plants of high mountains and temperate lowlands worldwide are identical or closely related";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter XI, “Geographical Distribution”; section “Dispersal during the Glacial period co-extensive with the world”. Scope: flowering plants of temperate lowlands and tropical and temperate mountains"
}
